rounds.text:
{"juror":1,"says":"Look, he paid twelve grand, they tore out his kitchen, and then work stopped at week four of ten. The contract says nothing about supplier delays, so that's on the contractor. I'm leaning toward Mr. Okafor, but I want to go through the numbers step by step.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":2,"says":"The texts show one layout change in week two, not two, so Brandt's story already has a hole in it. But I want to see what the contract actually obligates her to do before I decide on damages. Leaning P on breach, not settled on the remedy.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":3,"says":"A promise is a promise, and ten weeks is what she signed. That poor man went five months without a kitchen! I do think she should be held to her word, though I'd like to hear what everyone thinks about the receipts.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":4,"says":"The facts that matter to me are that work stopped in week four and he had to pay someone else $29,000 to finish. Whatever the reason, the job didn't get done on time. I think the contractor breached.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":5,"says":"I want to hear from everybody before I settle on anything. Small contractors get squeezed by suppliers too, and she did do demolition and plumbing work. Not ready to pick a side yet.","vote":"U"}
{"juror":6,"says":"It sounds like she didn't finish on time, and the contract doesn't excuse supplier delays. I'm not sure about the money part though, so I'll listen to the others. For now I lean toward the homeowner.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":7,"says":"On breach, the contract fixed a ten-week completion and contains no supplier-delay clause, so the risk sits with the contractor. One layout change in week two is thin grounds for claiming the schedule was voided. The harder question is the remedy: restitution of the deposit, or expectation damages, and these shouldn't be double-counted. I vote P on breach.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":8,"says":"It seems like she stopped working and left him without a kitchen for months. I'm not sure how the money should work, but I think she broke the agreement. I lean toward the plaintiff.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":9,"says":"I've run a contracting business for twenty years, and supplier delays happen, but you tell the customer and you put it in writing. She says he changed the layout twice and the texts show once, which sounds like spin to me. Still, she did real demo and plumbing work, so I don't think she owes back every dollar.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":10,"says":"A family lived five months without a working kitchen, and that human impact matters. A contractor holds the power in this relationship and should have communicated about delays. I think there was a breach, and I'm still thinking about the remedy.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":11,"says":"Let me look at the numbers. He paid $12,000 down plus $29,000 to the new contractor, which is $41,000 against a $38,000 original price, so the extra cost is about $3,000 if you count it that way. She has $7,300 in receipts. I think there's a breach, but the money has to be balanced fairly.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":12,"says":"I want to stick to the evidence and the instructions. The plaintiff has the burden, and so far all I have is the contractor's explanation against the texts. I'm not ready to vote until we go through the contract and the proof of damages.","vote":"U"}

{"juror":1,"says":"Okay, let's take this in order. First, breach: ten weeks promised, work stopped at week four, and the contract has no supplier-delay clause. Second, remedy: what does he get back? Tyrone and Ellen, you're the two undecided, so I'd like to hear what you need on breach before we get to the money.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":12,"says":"Thank you, Marisol. My concern is the burden. Mr. Okafor has to show it's more likely than not that Bright Hearth breached. The contract promises ten weeks and is silent on supplier delays, and the work stopped. I'll grant that is a prima facie case. But Ms. Brandt says he changed the layout, and the texts show one change in week two. Does one change in week two excuse a three-month stall? I'd say probably not, but I want to hear it argued from the record.","vote":"U"}
{"juror":7,"says":"Ellen, that's the right question. Even if the week-two change were a modification, nothing in the record shows she told him it would extend the schedule, or that the change caused the stall. She blames the supplier, and the contract puts that risk on her. On the evidence breach is more likely than not. The remedy is where we need discipline.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":5,"says":"I hear that, Gregory. But I keep thinking she did the demo and the plumbing rough-in. That's real work he got the benefit of. I'm not saying she's right on the delay. I just don't want to treat her like she did nothing. Does anybody disagree that she did work?","vote":"U"}
{"juror":9,"says":"Tyrone, I agree she did work, and I said so earlier. As a plumber, I know rough-in isn't free. But that's a remedy issue, not a breach issue. She had a duty to tell him about the delay in writing and she didn't, as far as we can see. And she blamed two layout changes when the texts show one, which hurts her credibility on everything else.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":11,"says":"Here are the numbers. He paid $12,000 and then $29,000, so $41,000 total against a $38,000 contract. That's $3,000 over. If we also gave him his whole deposit back, he'd be paid twice for the same loss. Her receipts are $7,300. Those are costs she incurred, but we don't know that the other contractor used any of that demo or plumbing work. I think the fair number is his extra cost, about $3,000, not the full deposit on top.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":2,"says":"Samuel's math holds up, and it's the expectation measure: put him where he'd be if she'd finished. He'd have paid $38,000 and instead paid $41,000. So $3,000 makes him whole. Returning the $12,000 as well would put him ahead. We should pick one theory, not stack them. Is anyone arguing for the deposit back instead?","vote":"P"}
{"juror":12,"says":"Daniel, that helps. Looking at what's in front of us, there's a signed estimate, a payment, a work stoppage, and a replacement cost. The contractor's excuses aren't supported by the texts or the contract. I'm persuaded on breach by the evidence, not by sympathy. I'll vote P. I still want the damages tied to proof, and $29,000 is documented while the receipts are only her side.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":3,"says":"I'm glad we're being careful, Ellen. I still feel for Mr. Okafor, five months with no kitchen, but I see now that the deposit and the $29,000 overlap. Tyrone, would it help if we said she keeps credit for the work that actually got done, but she doesn't get to blame him for her delay?","vote":"P"}
{"juror":5,"says":"Margaret, that's closer to where I am. I was worried we'd just throw the book at her. If we're counting what he actually lost and not double-dipping, that's fair to both sides. On breach, with no clause and the texts showing one change, I'll go with P.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":1,"says":"Good, that's 12 on breach. Let me sum up the remedy for the next round: expectation damages, meaning his extra cost over the $38,000 contract, with no double recovery of the deposit. Her $7,300 in receipts get credited because that work benefited him. Gregory, will you check that framing against the instructions?","vote":"P"}

{"juror":1,"says":"P. Ten weeks promised, work stopped at week four, no supplier-delay clause. He gets his extra cost over the $38,000 contract, with no double recovery of the deposit, and her $7,300 in receipts gets credited.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":2,"says":"P. Breach is clear from the contract and the texts. Expectation damages put him where he'd be had she finished, and we shouldn't stack the deposit refund on top.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":3,"says":"P. She promised ten weeks and didn't deliver. I'm comfortable that he recovers what he actually lost, with credit to her for the work she really did.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":4,"says":"P. The job stopped in week four and he paid $29,000 to finish it. She breached, and the remedy should follow his actual extra cost.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":5,"says":"P. She breached, but I want her credited for the demo and rough-in. If we count only what he really lost, that's fair to both sides.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":6,"says":"P. The contract doesn't excuse supplier delays and the work stopped. The remedy reasoning from Samuel and Daniel makes sense to me.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":7,"says":"P. Breach is more likely than not: a fixed ten-week term, no supplier-delay clause, and one layout change that wasn't shown to cause the stall. Expectation damages with a credit for her documented work, and no double recovery, is a sound framing.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":8,"says":"P. She stopped working and broke the agreement. I agree he should get his actual extra cost and not be paid twice.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":9,"says":"P. Breach is plain, and her two-changes claim against one text hurts her credibility. She did real work, so she shouldn't owe back the whole deposit. Extra cost is the fair measure.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":10,"says":"P. He was left five months without a kitchen and she never communicated about the delay. I support the measured remedy of his extra cost, with credit for her work.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":11,"says":"P. Breach is established. $12,000 plus $29,000 is $41,000 against $38,000, so the fair figure is his extra cost, with no double recovery of the deposit.","vote":"P"}
{"juror":12,"says":"P. On the evidence, breach is more likely than not. Damages must be tied to proof: the $29,000 is documented, and her receipts are credited only to the extent they're substantiated.","vote":"P"}
